# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# StudyCore Generation API — environment
#
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. This file carries SECRETS and DEPLOYMENT endpoints
# only; behavioural configuration lives in config/*.yaml (see plan.md §3).
#
# Two exceptions, because brief §5 names them explicitly and verifies them:
# QUEUE_MAX_DEPTH and WORKER_CONCURRENCY are read from here first, with the
# config/default.yaml values as fallback defaults.
#
# `docker-compose up` works with this file unchanged — every provider defaults to
# a stub, so the pipeline runs end to end with no credentials and no spend.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000
HOST=0.0.0.0
CONFIG_DIR=config

# ── Queue (brief §5: configurable via .env, verified) ────────────────────────
# Maximum queued jobs before the queue is considered saturated. Submissions over
# this still queue rather than being rejected — graceful degradation, not 4xx.
QUEUE_MAX_DEPTH=1000
# Jobs one worker container processes concurrently. Scale *across* containers
# with `docker-compose up --scale worker=N`.
WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2

REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379

# ── Workspace ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared across workers so a requeued job resumes on a different container
# (plan.md §4). In compose this is a named volume mounted at the same path.
WORKSPACE_DIR=/workspace/jobs

# ── Object storage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Published artifacts are written to disk and served by the API. A hosted bucket
# is a new adapter behind ObjectStoragePort, not a config flag — see
# docs/architecture.md.
STORAGE_LOCAL_DIR=/workspace/artifacts
STORAGE_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000/artifacts

# ── LLM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `stub` needs no credentials and no spend — the pipeline still runs end to end
# and produces a real MP4. Two real options:
#
#   openai  — OpenAI chat completions. One key, works immediately. Processes
#             in the US, which was reviewed and accepted for this project.
#   gemini  — the first-party Gemini API (aistudio.google.com/apikey). One key,
#             no gcloud. Same two tiers, same prompts, same schemas — the
#             adapter translates them; see infrastructure/llm/geminiSchema.ts.
LLM_DRIVER=stub
#OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-your-openai-key       # only for LLM_DRIVER=openai
#GEMINI_API_KEY=your-gemini-key          # only for LLM_DRIVER=gemini or TTS_DRIVER=gemini

# Two tiers: the quality model reads the student's material, writes the
# source-locked script and judges the finished video; the volume model writes
# storyboard HTML, judges each scene and writes the quiz.
# Prefixed per provider: the names are not interchangeable, and a shared key is
# how a .env ends up asking OpenAI for a Gemini model.
OPENAI_MODEL_QUALITY=gpt-4.1
OPENAI_MODEL_VOLUME=gpt-4.1
GEMINI_MODEL_QUALITY=gemini-3.1-pro-preview
GEMINI_MODEL_VOLUME=gemini-3.7-flash
LLM_MAX_RETRIES=5
LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# ── Prompts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Plain Markdown in prompts/*.md — edit them directly, no rebuild needed.
# Point PROMPT_DIR elsewhere to A/B a whole prompt set.
PROMPT_DIR=prompts
# Re-read prompt files on every call: edit a prompt, rerun, see the difference.
PROMPT_HOT_RELOAD=false

# ── Speech ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   stub       — correctly-timed silence. The whole timing chain stays real, so
#                anchors, subtitles and segment planning are all still exercised
#                without spending anything.
#   openai     — reuses OPENAI_API_KEY, so the pipeline needs one credential.
#                OpenAI returns audio without word timings, so the adapter
#                recovers them by transcribing its own output — a second
#                per-minute charge, metered into the tts line of cost.json.
#   elevenlabs — returns per-character alignment with the audio, so timings are
#                authoritative rather than recovered. Needs its own key.
#   gemini     — reuses GEMINI_API_KEY. Returns raw PCM and no word timings, so
#                it borrows an aligner: local whisper.cpp when STT_DRIVER=whisper,
#                otherwise the OpenAI transcription endpoint if that key is set.
#                With neither, narration is still correct but reveals fall back
#                to inherited timing — the boot log says which one is in use.
TTS_DRIVER=stub
TTS_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# For TTS_DRIVER=gemini. Voice per slot works like OpenAI's: the slot's VOICE_*
# value is used when it names a Gemini prebuilt voice (Kore, Puck, Charon,
# Zephyr, Leda…), and otherwise the slot's gender default is substituted.
GEMINI_TTS_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts

# For TTS_DRIVER=openai. Voice per slot comes from its VOICE_* value when that
# names an OpenAI voice; anything else falls back to the slot's gender default.
OPENAI_TTS_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini-tts
OPENAI_TTS_ALIGN_MODEL=whisper-1

# For TTS_DRIVER=elevenlabs.
#ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=your-elevenlabs-key
ELEVENLABS_MODEL_ID=eleven_multilingual_v2
ELEVENLABS_OUTPUT_FORMAT=mp3_44100_128

# Speech-to-text, for audio uploads and YouTube without captions.
#
#   stub    — returns nothing, so an audio-only job correctly reports
#             INSUFFICIENT_CONTENT rather than inventing a transcript.
#   whisper — local whisper.cpp. Student audio never leaves the machine: the
#             cleanest GDPR position, and free. Needs the binary and a model.
# There is no hosted option: transcription is local or nothing.
STT_DRIVER=stub

# For STT_DRIVER=whisper. Build whisper.cpp and download a model:
#   git clone https://github.com/ggerganov/whisper.cpp && cd whisper.cpp && make
#   ./models/download-ggml-model.sh large-v3-turbo
WHISPER_BINARY=whisper-cli
WHISPER_MODEL_PATH=models/ggml-large-v3-turbo.bin
# Whisper is CPU-bound and competes with rendering on the same box.
WHISPER_THREADS=4

# A 3-hour lecture is in scope, so the ceiling is generous.
STT_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000

# ── Images on a board ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The `illustration` shape: a real photograph or a published scientific diagram,
# found by search, resized, inlined and credited — for a subject whose
# appearance *is* the information, where a box-and-arrow drawing throws away the
# part that teaches.
#
# There is no IMAGES_DRIVER. The feature is on when a library is reachable and
# off when none is: with no keys here, the script stage is never offered the
# shape and every board is drawn, exactly as before the feature existed. A
# request can still turn it off per job (features.images in POST /v1/generate).
#
#   unsplash  — photographs. Excellent objects, places and materials; no
#               diagrams. https://unsplash.com/developers
#   pexels    — the second stock library, for the subjects Unsplash misses.
#               https://www.pexels.com/api/
#   wikimedia — where the scientific diagrams actually are, and the only source
#               that answers `kind: diagram`. No key, so it takes an explicit
#               opt-in: turning it on means this service makes outbound searches.
#   generated — draws the board instead of finding it: the reference image is
#               handed to an image model and redrawn in the theme's palette, so
#               a picture sits on a hand-drawn board instead of on top of it.
#               Runs on GEMINI_API_KEY, and takes an explicit switch because
#               that key is usually already set for the text models — presence
#               alone would turn on a per-image charge nobody asked for.
#UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=your-unsplash-access-key
#PEXELS_API_KEY=your-pexels-key
WIKIMEDIA_IMAGES=false
IMAGE_GENERATION=false
GEMINI_IMAGE_MODEL=gemini-3-pro-image
IMAGE_GENERATION_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# ── Web search ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Serves two things: researching a topic before the script is written, and the
# `web_search` image source.
#
#   none   — no research, and no web images. The video is built only from what
#            the caller sent.
#   gemini — the `google_search` tool on GEMINI_API_KEY, so no new credential.
#            Answers research only: grounding returns pages, never images.
#   brave  — https://brave.com/search/api/. Answers both, and is what
#            `features.image_sources: ["web_search"]` needs.
#
# What a search returns is a list of URLs, never content: the pages are fetched
# by the ordinary extractor through the SSRF guard, chunked with provenance and
# cited exactly like an upload. That is what keeps a researched fact traceable.
WEB_SEARCH_DRIVER=none
#BRAVE_API_KEY=your-brave-key
WEB_SEARCH_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# Wikimedia's API policy requires a real contact in the User-Agent; requests
# without one are throttled or blocked.
IMAGE_USER_AGENT=StudyCoreGenerationApi/0.1 (+https://github.com/studycore)
IMAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# ── Renderer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is one renderer: Chromium via Playwright. It renders each scene's own
# HTML/CSS/SVG, so diagrams and motion are whatever the storyboard authored.
# The worker and e2e images install Chromium and set CHROMIUM_PATH; on a bare
# host, point it at your own binary.
#CHROMIUM_PATH=/usr/bin/chromium

# ── Voice slots (brief §4 names these keys explicitly) ───────────────────────
# Slot metadata is in config/voices.yaml; the provider voice id lives here, so
# swapping a voice never touches code. All four need Saman's approval at M1.
# One key per slot, holding whichever provider TTS_DRIVER names. The two
# providers identify voices differently and a key cannot hold both, so these
# values must match the driver:
#
#   openai     — public voice names, stable, no lookup needed. A value that is
#                *not* one of the names below is treated as another vendor's id
#                and silently replaced by the slot's gender default, so a typo
#                here reads as "my voice setting did nothing".
#                alloy ash ballad cedar coral echo fable marin onyx nova sage
#                shimmer verse
#   elevenlabs — account-visible ids that can change; `npm run voices` lists
#                what your key can actually see.
#
# The defaults below are OpenAI names chosen for narration: warm and measured
# rather than characterful. The voices are multilingual — one voice speaks both
# en and es — so the Spanish slots are a different *voice*, not a different
# language build.
VOICE_EN_FEMALE_1=marin
VOICE_EN_MALE_1=cedar
VOICE_ES_FEMALE_1=coral
VOICE_ES_MALE_1=ash
# For TTS_DRIVER=elevenlabs, use ids instead — e.g.
#VOICE_EN_FEMALE_1=21m00Tcm4TlvDq8ikWAM
#VOICE_EN_MALE_1=pNInz6obpgDQGcFmaJgB
#VOICE_ES_FEMALE_1=EXAVITQu4vr4xnSDxMaL
#VOICE_ES_MALE_1=ErXwobaYiN019PkySvjV
